###Question: Concerning Hereditary Spherocytosis:
###Options:
A.  a.	It is an autosomal dominant disorder;
B.  b.	Splenomegaly is present majority of patients;
C.  c.	Leg Ulcer is a recognized complication;
D.  d.	Splenectomy is contraindicated;
E.  e.	Osmotic Fragility test is reduced.

###Answer:

outputs:
 A. a.	It is an autosomal dominant disorder;

###Rationale: 
Hereditary spherocytosis (HS) is a genetic disorder of the red blood cell membrane characterized by the production of abnormally shaped red blood cells (spherocytes) that are prone to hemolysis. The majority of cases of hereditary spherocytosis are inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern, which means that a single copy of the mutated gene is enough to cause the condition.  One mutated copy of the gene is enough to cause the condition, and if one parent has the condition, each child has a 50% chance of inheriting the mutated gene. This explains why Hereditary Spherocytosis is predominantly an autosomal dominant disorder.

